+## Team Communication
+
+Communication is off by default. Enable it on one parallel `subagent` call with `communication: true`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "communication": true,
+  "tasks": [
+    {
+      "id": "interfaces",
+      "agent": "worker",
+      "task": "Inspect the storage interface. Share constraints with implementation as you discover them."
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "implementation",
+      "agent": "worker",
+      "task": "Implement the storage adapter. Use findings from interfaces and share approaches you rule out."
+    }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+Each task has its own address, even when both use `worker`. IDs default to `worker-1`, `worker-2`, etc., by position in `tasks`. Explicit IDs must be unique within the dispatch, use 1–64 ASCII letters, digits, underscores or hyphens, and start with a letter or digit. Duplicate IDs, including collisions with generated IDs, are rejected. Communication with single or chain mode is rejected.
+
+Workers still run as separate Pi processes. The same limit of eight tasks and four concurrent processes applies. A worker is `queued` before spawning, `starting` during initialization, `running` after joining the team, and `finished` after retirement or exit. Only `running` teammates accept findings. A roster is a snapshot, not a reservation: check every send result.
+
+### Worker Tools
+
+The parent automatically loads the worker extension and adds these tools to an agent's explicit tool allowlist. Existing agent tools, system prompt, model selection, thinking level, working directory, and other extensions remain in use. Without communication, no worker extension, IPC channel, or team tools are added.
+
+| Tool | Arguments | Result |
+|------|-----------|--------|
+| `team_list` | `{}` | `{ "kind": "roster", "self": "interfaces", "members": [{ "id", "agent", "task", "status" }, ...] }` |
+| `team_send` | `{ "to": "implementation", "text": "The adapter must support AbortSignal." }` | `{ "kind": "delivery", "accepted": ["implementation"], "failed": [] }` |
+| `team_broadcast` | `{ "text": "The legacy endpoint cannot support this approach." }` | Per-recipient `accepted` and `failed` lists |
+
+Results appear as JSON text and in tool details. `team_list` includes the caller so it can discover its own identity. Broadcast excludes the caller and reports non-running teammates as unsuccessful rather than queueing for their future startup. Private sends reject the caller, unknown IDs, and non-running teammates.
+
+For example, a broadcast can partially succeed:
+
+```json
+{
+  "kind": "delivery",
+  "accepted": ["implementation"],
+  "failed": [
+    { "to": "review", "reason": "Teammate is finished" },
+    { "to": "tests", "reason": "Teammate is queued" }
+  ]
+}
+```
+
+An accepted recipient must not be retried just because another recipient failed: a second send is a new message. Sending waits only for broker acceptance, never for the recipient's tools, model, acknowledgment, or reply. Successful acceptance is not a claim that the finding has already been read.
+
+Messages must be non-empty strings of at most **65,536 UTF-8 bytes**. Unicode, line breaks, and complete text are preserved, not truncated. Each recipient can queue up to **256 findings or 1 MiB of text**, whichever limit comes first. A full inbox produces an explicit per-recipient failure; invalid requests and oversized messages return `{ "kind": "error", "error": "..." }`. These limits are independent of the parent-facing 50 KB final-output cap.
+
+### Delivery And Lifecycle
+
+The parent owns an in-memory broker for each dispatch. Each child gets a private inherited Node IPC channel; there is no shared socket address, environment credential, mailbox file, or persistent state. Sender identity comes from the child's channel, not from model-supplied arguments. Private findings enter only the chosen teammate's context; the parent can still inspect normal subagent tool traces. This is dispatch isolation, not a sandbox against hostile local code.
+
+Workers automatically drain accepted findings at `turn_end`, after the entire model response and all its tools finish. Findings are queued together as one custom steering message, so the default one-at-a-time steering mode does not split a delivery batch across model calls. No tool is interrupted and no model needs to call an inbox tool. Each finding contains the actual worker ID, agent name, and full text. During normal execution, each accepted finding is delivered once, in each sender's order to each recipient.
+
+Finishing requires a second, atomic broker handshake at `agent_end`. For example, if a teammate sends after the final response's `turn_end` drain but before retirement, the handshake returns that finding and Pi continues for another model call. Only an empty final drain marks the worker finished. Sends and retirement run serially in the broker, so a send either reserves delivery or reports a finished recipient; later sends never restart the worker.
+
+Cancelling the dispatch or shutting down the parent closes acceptance, terminates its children (with a forced-kill fallback), waits for them, and releases IPC resources. Cancellation and abnormal process/provider failures may abandon outstanding work; crash recovery is not provided. Concurrent teams and later dispatches have independent brokers and channels, even when they reuse the same IDs. Cross-session messaging, nested-team coordination, persistent shared memory, and a separate messaging UI are not provided.
+
